  "question": {
    "text": "A particle of mass <math><mi>m</mi></math> moves in the <math><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> plane with a velocity <math><mi>v</mi></math> along the straight line <math><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math>. If the angular momentum of the particle with respect to origin <math><mi>O</mi></math> is <math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></math> when it is at <math><mi>A</mi></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></math> when it is at <math><mi>B</mi></math>, then<br />\r<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/WhatsApp_Image_2020-06-30_at_4.56.30_PM.jpeg\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 145px;\" />",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "eakDd",
      "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "x0RFp",
      "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "2GaA2",
      "text": "The relationship between <math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></math> depends upon the slope of the line <math><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "euJKl",
      "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "From the definition of angular momentum,<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/WhatsApp_Image_2020-06-30_at_5.01.27_PM.jpeg\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 139px;\" /><br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∅</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></math><br /><br />Therefore, the magnitude of <math><mi>L</mi></math> is<br /><br /><math><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>∅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></math><br /><br />where <math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>∅</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the distance of closest approach of the particle to the origin. As <math><mi>d</mi></math> is same for both the particles, hence <math><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></math>.",
    "image": null
  },
